<topic xml:id="packages-lifecycle-virtualization"
       role="concept" xml:lang="en"
       xmlns="http://docbook.org/ns/docbook" version="5.2"
       xmlns:its="http://www.w3.org/2005/11/its"
       xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude"
       xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"
       xmlns:trans="http://docbook.org/ns/transclusion">
    <info>
        <title>Virtualization components update strategy</title>
        <meta name="maintainer" content="daria.vladykina@suse.com" its:translate="no"/>
        <abstract>
            <para>This section documents the lifecycle management and update strategy for virtualization
                components in &suselinux;&nbsp;16.</para>
        </abstract>
    </info>
    <section xml:id="sec-sles-virt-included-components">
        <title>Included components and target versions</title>
        <para>
            &suselinux;&nbsp;16 provides a modern virtualization stack centered around KVM and QEMU. The
            primary shipped components and their initial target versions include:
        </para>
        <itemizedlist xml:id="itemlist-virt-shipped">
            <listitem>
                <para>
                    <package>KVM</package>: Inherited directly from the Linux kernel lifecycle.
                </para>
            </listitem>
            <listitem>
                <para>
                    <package>QEMU</package>: Target version 9.2.0.
                </para>
            </listitem>
            <listitem>
                <para>
                    <package>libvirt</package>: Target version 11.0.0.
                </para>
            </listitem>
            <listitem>
                <para>
                    <package>open-vm-tools</package>: Target version 12.5.0.</para>
            </listitem>
            <listitem>
                <para>
                    <package>snpguest</package>: Target version 0.7.1 (utility for AMD SEV-SNP guest
                    administration).
                </para>
            </listitem>
            <listitem>
                <para>
                    <package>virt-top</package>: Target version 1.1.1.
                </para>
            </listitem>
            <listitem>
                <para>
                    <package>virt-manager</package>: Desktop guest management application.
                </para>
            </listitem>
            <listitem>
                <para>
                    <package>Hyper-V</package>: Version 9 guest enlightenments and driver stack.
                </para>
            </listitem>
        </itemizedlist>
    </section>
    <section xml:id="sec-sles-virt-excluded-components">
        <title>Components not shipped in &suselinux;&nbsp;16</title>
        <para>
            To streamline the virtualization offering and focus on core hypervisor technologies, the
            following legacy or deprecated components are <emphasis>not shipped</emphasis> in
            &suselinux;&nbsp;16:
        </para>
        <itemizedlist xml:id="itemlist-virt-excluded">
            <listitem>
                <para>
                    Xen hypervisor (<package>xen</package>)
                </para>
            </listitem>
            <listitem>
                <para>
                    SPICE protocol (<package>spice</package>)</para>
            </listitem>
            <listitem>
                <para>
                    RADOS block device support in QEMU and libvirt
                </para>
            </listitem>
            <listitem>
                <para>
                    <command>vm-install</command>
                </para>
            </listitem>
            <listitem>
                <para>
                    Migration tools <command>virt-v2v</command> and <command>virt-p2v</command>
                        </para>
                    </listitem>
                    <listitem>
                        <para>
                            &yast; virtualization module (<package>yast2-vm</package>)
                        </para>
                    </listitem>
                </itemizedlist>
            </section>
            <section xml:id="sec-sles-virt-lifecycle-policy">
                <title>Lifecycle model and update policy</title>
                <para>
                    Virtualization components follow an agile update strategy aligned with &suselinux;&nbsp; minor
                    releases, backed by continuous maintenance support:
                </para>
                <variablelist xml:id="vlist-virt-policy">
                    <varlistentry>
                        <term>Minor release upgrades</term>
                        <listitem>
                            <para>
                                Every new &suselinux;&nbsp;16 minor release is updated to the latest available upstream
                                versions for all supported virtualization packages.
                            </para>
                        </listitem>
                    </varlistentry><varlistentry>
                        <term>Support across lifecycles</term>
                        <listitem>
                            <para>
                                Supported across all &suselinux;&nbsp;16 minor releases throughout both the General Support
                                and LTSS phases.
                            </para>
                        </listitem>
                    </varlistentry>
                    <varlistentry>
                        <term>Intra-release maintenance updates</term>
                        <listitem>
                            <para>
                                In exceptional cases where packages maintain strict backward compatibility, version
                                updates may be delivered within an already released &suselinux;&nbsp; minor codestream via
                                maintenance updates.
                            </para>
                        </listitem>
                    </varlistentry>
                    <varlistentry>
                        <term>Security patching</term>
                        <listitem>
                            <para>
                                Security fixes are backported as necessary when upstream does not issue a
                                prompt point release containing the fix.
                            </para>
                        </listitem>
                    </varlistentry>
                    <varlistentry>
                        <term>Automated quality assurance</term>
                        <listitem>
                            <para>
                                Automated testing is in place for all key enterprise use cases across critical
                                combinations of host and guest operating systems.
                            </para>
                        </listitem>
                    </varlistentry>
                </variablelist>
            </section>
</topic>
